<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A JSON Web Token (JWT) has three base64url-encoded segments separated by dots: a header, the
claims, and a signature. Some code extracts the claims by hand, by splitting the token on
<code>"."</code>, base64-decoding the second segment and parsing it as JSON, without ever checking
the third (signature) segment. Since anyone can encode arbitrary claims, an attacker can then
impersonate any user, for example an administrator.
</p>
<p>
This query flags such hand-written decoding when the signature segment of the split token is
never read. It is a heuristic, so code that verifies the token elsewhere (for example in an API
gateway authorizer) may be reported.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Decode tokens with a JWT library such as PyJWT, passing the expected key and an explicit list of
allowed algorithms, so that the signature is verified before any claim is trusted. If the token is
verified by another component, document that and make sure the handler cannot be reached without it.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, the acting user is taken from the claims segment of the
<code>Authorization</code> header without verifying the signature:
</p>
<sample src="examples/bad.py" />
<p>
The fix is to decode the token with a library that verifies the signature:
</p>
<sample src="examples/good.py" />
</example>

<references>
<li>CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/347.html">CWE-347: Improper Verification of Cryptographic Signature</a>.</li>
<li>IETF: <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7519#section-7.2">RFC 7519, Section 7.2: Validating a JWT</a>.</li>
<li>PyJWT: <a href="https://pyjwt.readthedocs.io/en/stable/usage.html">Usage Examples</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
